## 1. Introduction
Exponential increase in heavy metal utilization have caused a critical challenge for the public health and water ecosystems [1– 3]. Lead (Pb) and copper (Cu) with high toxicity and nonbiodegradability could cause severe damage to the human body through drinking water, such as multiple organ dysfunction and birth defects [4–6]. Serious heavy metal pollution incidents occurred occasionally, for example, lead wastewater from Shanxi Dongling smelting plant caused excessive blood lead in 4035 people with 32 serious casualties [7]. The leakage of copper sewage from the Zijin mine caused 1.89 tons fish poisoned, resulting in the accumulation of copper in human bodies due to biomagnification [8]. Currently, a series of methods have been developed for heavy metal removal from water, including ion exchange [9], chemical precipitation [10], adsorption [11], electrolysis [12] and membrane filtration [13]. Among diverse approaches, adsorption has been most widely employed due to its low cost, simplicity, versatility, and high removal efficiency [14–16]. However, traditional adsorbents (e.g., zeolites [17], clays [18] and activated carbons [19]) are usually restricted for the low adsorption rate and capacity as well as poor selectivity for metal ions with similar valence states. Recently, metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) have turned into promising adsorbents in heavy metal removal for the high surface area, rich microstructures, abundant active sites, and diverse functionality [20–23].
Among diversiform MOFs, zeolitic imidazolate frameworks-8 (ZIF-8), constructed by tetrahedral $Z\mathrm{n}$ -clusters and 2- methylimidazole ligands, demonstrated numerous merits such as abundant nitrogen atoms sites and relatively high hydrophilicity, which make it promising for capturing Pb(II) and $\mathtt{C u(I I)}$ from water [24–26]. Alcohols (e.g., methanol [27], ethanol [28]) were commonly utilized as solvents for the nucleation, resulting in threedimensional rhombic dodecahedral ZIF-8. In contrast, the aqueous synthesis of ZIF-L [29] with two-dimensional leaf-like morphology is more favorable in terms of economy, sustainability, operability, and high-yield production [30,31]. Though the pristine ZIF- $_{8/\mathrm{L}}$ showed much higher adsorption capacities than most traditional sorbents, the adsorption performance were still far from expectations [32–34]. Moreover, deep removal of heavy metal ions to below maximum contamination level (MCL) for drinking water (e.g., $<0.015~\mathrm{mg/L}$ for Pb(II), ${<}1.3\ \mathrm{mg/L}$ for $\mathtt{C u(I I)}$ , referred to U.S. Environmental Protection Agency (EPA) [35]) remains a great challenge. Therefore, the exploration of methods to improve performance of ZIFs for effective heavy metal removal remains highly desired for supplying clean water resources.
To date, the structure and defect engineering have been regarded as effective strategies to introduce specific surface functional groups and manipulate unsaturated defects, for the development of high-performance adsorbents [36]. The nitrogencontaining groups were introduced to the ZIF-derived carbon by modification with ammonia for enhanced acetone removal that increased by $17.4\%$ [37]. Defect-rich Zn-ZIF adsorbents were synthesized with ZIF-L as precursor to achieve ${\sim}1.4$ folds of phosphate adsorption [38]. Whereas, these methods usually suffer from complicated synthesis procedure, and few researches focus on promoting heavy metal adsorption via morphology engineering. Generally, the morphology evolution of ZIFs could be realized by varying the solvent conditions. It is reported that the rhombic dodecahedral ZIF-8 can be mutually transformed to leaf-shaped ZIF-L by adjusting the ratio of methanol/water or polyvinylpyrrolidone/ethanol [39,40]. Note that the morphology regulation of ZIFs by ultrasonication treatment is scarcely reported. In addition, the developed high-performance MOFs with powder form generally subject to limited stability and poor separability, hindering their actual applications in water matrixes. Flourishing researches focused on constructing ZIF-L-based composites (e.g., ZIF- $\cdot\mathrm{L}/\$ aerogel [41,42], ZIF-L/carbon [43,44], and ZIF-L/membranes [45,46]) by in-situ or post-treatment methods. However, the now available approaches are limited by poor mechanical properties and complex synthesis processes, as well as uneven loading. Therefore, it remains a challenge to develop a facile method to construct macroscopical ZIF-L material with high stability and uniformity to strengthen its applicability in heavy metal removal from water. Melamine sponge (MS) stands out as ideal support for ZIF-L owing to its interconnected networks, and high porosity, stability and flexibility [47]. It is hypothesized that the superior adsorption performance of ZIF-L could be realized by incorporating ZIF-L onto MS networks.
In this work, a series of macroscopic ZIF-L-functionalized melamine sponges $(\mathrm{MS-ZIF_{x}})$ were prepared via a facile dip-coating method for the efficient $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ and $\mathsf{C u}(\mathrm{II})$ removal from water. We presented the morphology and structure modulation using varying ultrasonication duration for enhanced heavy metal adsorption, which could manipulate the morphology from leaf shape to hollow ring shape accompanied by a phase transformation from ZIF-L to mixed phase. Apparently, taking advantage of the unique morphology and multiphase structure, the optimized $\mathrm{MS}{-}Z\mathrm{IF}_{10}$ exhibited outstanding performance for Pb(II) and $\mathtt{C u(I I)}$ removal in adsorption capacity, kinetics, anti-interfering ability, recyclability as well as applicability in real water matrices. Moreover, the adsorption mechanisms were elucidated through various physicochemical characterizations and the continuous performance of the MS$Z\mathrm{IF}_{10}$ was further evaluated via fix-bed column adsorption tests.
## 2. Experimental sections
## 2.1. Synthesis of ZIF-L
Generally, $6.0\ g\ Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ was dissolved in $200~\mathrm{{mL}}$ deionized water (DIW). Then $200~\mathrm{\mL}$ of DIW containing $14.0\mathrm{~g~}2\AA$ Methylimidazole was added to the above $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{II})$ -contained solution. The mixture was under mechanical agitation at 25 rpm, $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^\mathrm{~2~h~}$ to form milky solution. The molar ratio of $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ : 2- methylimidazole: $_{\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}}$ in the above solution was ${\sim}1{:}8.5{:}1100$ . The white precipitates were collected via centrifugation, followed by 2 times of DIW washing and 1 time of methanol washing. The final products were freeze-dried for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ to obtain ZIF-L.
