## 2.3. Batch adsorption tests
Unless otherwise specified, all batch adsorption experiments were performed in a mechanical shaker at $180~\mathrm{rpm}$ , $25^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ . Certain amounts of $\mathsf{P b}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}$ or $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}.3\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ powders were dissolved into DIW to prepare the $1000\ \mathrm{\mg/L\Pb(II)}$ and $\mathtt{C u(I I)}$ stock solutions, which could be diluted to obtain $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ and $\mathtt{C u(I I)}$ solutions at various desired concentrations. Desired initial solution $\mathsf{p H}$ values were adjusted with $0.1\ \mathrm{\mol/L}\ \mathrm{\HNO}_{3}$ or NaOH. The initial concentrations $(\mathsf{C}_{0})$ of $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ and $\mathtt{C u(I I)}$ in the kinetics ranged from 10 to $40~\mathrm{mg/L}$ with adsorbent dosages of $1\mathrm{~g~L~}^{-1}$ . The ${\sf C}_{0}$ of $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ and $\mathtt{C u(I I)}$ in the adsorption isotherms ranged from 20 to $500~\mathrm{\mg/L}$ with adsorbent dosages of $0.5\mathrm{~g~L^{-1}~}$ . The influences of interfering ions and dissolved organic matter were studied by addition of metal salts (NaCl, KCl, $\mathsf{C a C l}_{2}$ , $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{MgCl}_{2}}$ ) and humic acid in the batch experiments. To characterize the competitive sorption and sorption selectivity of $\mathrm{MS}{-}\mathrm{ZIF}_{10}$ , the tests were performed in the presence of individual metal salts (e.g., ${\mathsf{N a N O}}_{3}$ , ${\mathsf{K N O}}_{3}$ , $\mathsf{M g}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}$ , $\mathsf{C a}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}$ , Zn $(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}$ , $\mathsf{C d}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}$ , $\mathsf{N i}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2})$ and mixture of all above metal salts into the $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ or $\mathtt{C u(I I)}$ solution. The $\mathsf{p H}$ influence experiments were conducted under various initial pH (1–6). In the cycle tests, the ${\sf C}_{0}$ of $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ and $\mathtt{C u(I I)}$ were $25~\mathrm{mg/L}$ with adsorbent dosages of $1.0\ \mathrm{g}\ \mathrm{L}^{-1}$ . The procedure was repeated 10 times with $0.1\mathrm{\mol/L}$ HCl as eluting solution. Each reaction time was $^\textrm{\scriptsize4h}$ and regeneration time was $^\mathrm{~8~h~}$ . To evaluate the performance of ${\tt M S-Z I F}_{10}$ in actual water scenarios, adsorption tests were carried out in the simulated wastewater by adding $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ or $\mathtt{C u(I I)}$ to real water matrices (e.g. groundwater, surface water and tap water). Note that the groundwater and surface water were sampled from Baihe Spring of Yuelu Mountain and Xiangjiang River, respectively, and the concentrations of typical cations in the used real water samples were shown in Table S2. The residual solution was filtered using a $0.22~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ polyether sulfone syringe membrane. The experimental details of adsorption experiments were summarized in each figure captions. All the experimental data were the averages of duplicate determinations. $\ensuremath{\mathrm{MS}}\ensuremath{-}\ensuremath{\mathrm{ZIF}_{10}}$ absorbed $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ and $\mathtt{C u(I I)}$ were denoted as ${\mathsf{M S}}–{\mathsf{Z I F}}_{10}–{\mathsf{P b}}$ and $\mathsf{M S-Z I F}_{10^{-}}\mathsf{C u}$ , respectively.
## 2.4. Fixed-bed experiments
The fixed-bed experiments were performed using ${\sim}1.0~\mathrm{g}$ MS$Z\mathrm{IF}_{10}$ $(1.5\times1.5\times1.5\ \mathrm{mm}^{3}$ in size) packed in plexiglass columns ( $12~\mathrm{mm}$ in diameter, $100~\mathrm{{mm}}$ in length). Both ends of the column were filled with quartz cotton ( $\mathord{\sim}10\mathrm{mm}$ thick) for filtration and dispersion, and the effective bed volume was ${\sim}9.04\mathrm{mL}$ . The empty bed contact time (EBCT) was set as 7.5 min. The constant upward flow rate of solution was $1.2~\mathrm{mL}~\mathrm{min}^{-1}$ maintained by the peristaltic pump. $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ -spiked DIW $(\mathsf C_{0}=3.073~\mathrm{mg/L})$ and $\mathtt{C u(I I)}$ -spiked DIW $(\mathsf C_{0}=5.039\mathrm{mg/L})$ were used as the feeding solutions.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Characterization of $M S-Z I F_{x}\left(x=5,10,15\:m i n\right)$
Fig. S1a illustrated the schematic for the fabrication of the macroscopic ZIF functionalized sponges. Primarily, the pristine ZIF-L synthesized in aqueous solution showed a two-dimensional leaf-shaped morphology with the length of $4{\div}9\upmu\mathrm{m}$ and the thickness of ${\sim}600\mathrm{nm}$ , which can stack into flower-like structures due to the hydrogen bonding interactions [48] (Fig. 1b and Fig. S2a-b). To enhance the dispersity of ZIF-L particles, ultrasonication treatment with varying processing duration (5, 10, and $15~\mathrm{\min};$ was employed (note that ZIF-L without ultrasonication could not be directly incorporated into MS due to the severe particles agglomeration (Figure S3)). The loading ratio of ZIF-L on the resultant MS$Z\mathrm{IF_{x}}$ $\mathbf{\dot{x}}$ represents different ultrasonication duration) was determined to be $\sim40\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ (see Table S1 for details). Moreover, the $\ensuremath{\mathrm{MS}}\ensuremath{-}\ensuremath{\mathrm{ZIF}_{10}}$ exhibited high hydroscopicity with the water absorption rate $(\mathsf{W}_{\mathsf{A}})$ (see Text S3 for specific calculations) of $1875.15\ \%$ (Table S3), which was higher than that of MS-ZIF5 ( $1181.30\ \%$ and $\mathsf{M S-Z I F}_{15}$ ( $1505.40\%$ .
To further investigate the effects of ultrasonication duration on morphology evolution, the resultant $\ensuremath{\mathrm{MS}}\ensuremath{-}\ensuremath{\mathrm{ZIF}_{\mathrm{x}}}$ were characterized using scanning electron microscopy (SEM) and transmission electron microscope (TEM). After being incorporated into $\mathrm{MS-ZIF_{x},}$ the lamellar size of the ZIF-L gradually decreased with the ultrasonication processing, meanwhile the original networks of pristine MS were well preserved (Fig. S1b-d). The ZIF-L on the ${\mathrm{MS}}–Z{\mathrm{IF}}_{5}$ networks remained leaf-shaped morphology ${.\sim}4\upmu\mathrm{m}$ in length and $\sim500\mathrm{nm}$ in thickness) with obvious stacking phenomenon (Fig. 1c). As for the ZIF-L on the $\ensuremath{\mathrm{MS}}\ensuremath{-}\ensuremath{\mathrm{ZIF}_{10}}$ networks, the unique hollow ringlike ZIF-L particles were observed with the length of $\sim3\upmu\mathrm{m}$ and the thickness of $\sim420\mathrm{nm}$ (Fig. 1d and Fig. S2c-d), which could greatly increase the surface area of the ZIF-L particles, and conceivably accessibility of the adsorption sites. Fig. S4 showed the cross-section SEM images at different locations and scales of inner networks of $\ensuremath{\mathrm{MS}}\ensuremath{-}\ensuremath{\mathrm{ZIF}_{10}}$ , indicating the uniform distribution of ZIF-L particles onto the MS networks and further confirming the hollow ring shape of $\ensuremath{\mathrm{MS}}\ensuremath{-}\ensuremath{\mathrm{ZIF}_{10}}$ However, the ZIF-L that underwent the ultrasonication for $10~\mathrm{{min}}$ while not loaded on the MS (denoted as ZIF-L-US) presented the morphology of irregular fragments (Fig. S5a-b). This suggested that the hollow ZIF-L ring was formed during the following dippingcoating procedure including DMF volatilization that could induce the morphology transformation of ZIF-L [49]. Upon ultrasonication treatment for $15\mathrm{min}$ , the ZIF-L on the ${\tt M S-Z I F}_{15}$ networks appeared as irregular fragments with the length of ${\sim}2\upmu\mathrm{m}$ and the thickness of ${\sim}320~\mathrm{nm}$ (Fig. 1e). Moreover, the surface area and porosity of the $\ensuremath{\mathrm{MS}}\ensuremath{-}\ensuremath{\mathrm{ZIF}_{\mathrm{x}}}$ were determined using Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) method (Fig. S6). The typical tyle IV hysteresis loops indicated the mesoporous structures of the $\ensuremath{\mathrm{MS}}\ensuremath{-}\ensuremath{\mathrm{ZIF}_{\mathrm{x}}}$ , which was ascribed to the incorporation of ZIF-L into the MS networks [50]. The specific surface area of $\ensuremath{\mathrm{MS}}\ensuremath{-}\ensuremath{\mathrm{ZIF}_{10}}$ was determined to be $1492.27{\mathrm{~m}}^{2}{\mathrm{~g}}^{-1}{\mathrm{~}}$ (normalized by the MOF loading ratio owing to the negligible surface areas of MS $(0.31\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1})_{.}^{.}$ ) (Table S4), which was almost 3.9–5.0 times that of the MS-ZIF5 $\mathbf{\zeta}_{381.04}\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathbf{g}^{-1},$ and $\mathrm{MS-}\mathrm{ZIF}_{15}$ $(295.98\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}.\nonumber$ ). It is worth mentioned that the surface area of ZIF-L-US was merely-one ninth of the pristine ZIF-L $(203.58~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}.$ ), which may result from the structural collapse and porosity damage caused by ultrasonication [51]. The above results suggested the crucial role of the ultrasonication duration in modulating the morphology and porosity of the loaded ZIF-L particles.
X-ray diffraction (XRD) technique was further employed to scrutinize the structure evolution of the $\ensuremath{\mathrm{MS}}\ensuremath{-}\ensuremath{\mathrm{ZIF}_{\mathrm{x}}}$ with the prolonging ultrasonication duration. As displayed in Fig. 1f, the pristine ZIF-L exhibited the characteristic peaks at $10.8^{\circ}$ , $15.0^{\circ}$ , $17.1^{\circ}$ and $18.0^{\circ}$ , corresponding to the (200), (22 0), (31 0) and (22 2) crystal planes of the simulated ZIF-L phase [29,52]. These characteristic peaks also presented in the XRD patterns of the ${\mathrm{MS}}{-}Z{\mathrm{IF}}_{5}$ and MS$Z\mathrm{IF}_{15}$ . The relatively weak diffraction peaks at $7.4^{\circ}$ and $12.7^{\circ}$ derived from the ZIF-8 phase [53] were also observed in the XRD patterns of the ${\mathrm{MS}}–Z{\mathrm{IF}}_{5}$ and $\mathsf{M S}{-}Z\mathsf{I F}_{15}$ . These results indicated the coexistence of ZIF-L and ZIF-8 phases in the resultant ${\mathrm{MS}}{-}Z{\mathrm{IF}}_{5} $ and $\mathrm{MS-}\mathrm{ZIF}_{15}$ , with ZIF-L as the prominent phase. Meanwhile, both the characteristic peaks of ZIF-L and ZIF-8 were observed in the XRD pattern of the $\ensuremath{\mathrm{MS}}\ensuremath{-}\ensuremath{\mathrm{ZIF}_{10}}$ , while ZIF-8 showed as the prominent phase. These observations demonstrated the occurrence of the phase transformation during the morphology evolution, which might be owing to the bond fracture or the formation of excited molecules in ZIF structures [54]. Therefore, it can be concluded that both the morphologies and crystal structures of the ZIF-L on MS$Z\mathrm{IF_{x}}$ were closely dependent on the ultrasonication duration.
## 3.2. Enhanced heavy metal adsorption by modulating micro morphology and structure
## 3.2.1. Adsorption isotherms
The isotherms experiments were performed to determine the theoretical maximum adsorption capacity $(q_{m})$ of ZIF-L before and after incorporation into MS. Langmuir and Freundlich models were employed to describe the adsorption behaviors, and relevant parameters were provided in Table S5. Comparing the correlation coefficients, the isotherm data of $\ensuremath{\mathrm{MS}}\ensuremath{-}\ensuremath{\mathrm{ZIF}_{10}}$ was better fitted using
Langmuir model than Freundlich model, indicating the monolayer adsorption process [55]. Additionally, the calculated free energy (E) of $\ensuremath{\mathbf{M}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{S}}-\ensuremath{\mathrm{ZIF}_{10}}$ based on the Dubinin-Radushkevish (D-R) isotherm [56] modeling was 11.077 and $13.378~\mathrm{kJ~mol}^{-1}$ for $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ and $\mathtt{C u}$ (II) (Fig. S7), which were in the energy range of the chemisorption process $(8{-}16\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1};$ ) [20]. As shown in Fig. 2a-b, the pristine MS showed negligible adsorption for both $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ and $\mathtt{C u(I I)}$ , therefore the $q_{m}$ values of $\ensuremath{\mathrm{MS}}\ensuremath{-}\ensuremath{\mathrm{ZIF}_{10}}$ for $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ and $\mathtt{C u(I I)}$ adsorption were normalized by the MOF loading ratio. The $q_{m}$ of $\ensuremath{\mathrm{MS}}\ensuremath{-}\ensuremath{\mathrm{ZIF}_{10}}$ were determined to be 624.8 and $588.6~\mathrm{mg~g^{-1}}$ for $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ and $\mathtt{C u(I I)}$ , which were ${\sim}6.2$ and ${\sim}1.1$ times higher than those onto pristine ZIF-L and ZIF-L-US (Fig. S8). To unveil the effects of morphologies and structures discrepancies on the adsorption capability, the adsorption isotherms of the resultant $\ensuremath{\mathrm{MS}}\ensuremath{-}\ensuremath{\mathrm{ZIF}_{\mathrm{x}}}$ were further studied (Table S6). As illustrated in Fig. S9a, the $q_{m}$ for $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ onto MS$Z\mathrm{IF}_{10}$ was ${\sim}1.6$ and ${\sim}1.9$ times higher than that of ${\mathrm{MS}}{\mathrm{-}}Z{\mathrm{IF}}_{5}$ $(398.3\ \mathrm{mg\d^{-1}}$ ) and $\mathsf{M S-Z I F}_{15}$ $326.2\ \mathrm{mg\g^{-1}}$ ), respectively. The $q_{m}$ for $\mathtt{C u(I I)}$ onto ${\mathrm{MS}}{\mathrm{-}}Z{\mathrm{IF}}_{10}$ was ${\sim}1.4$ and ${\sim}1.7$ times higher than that of ${\mathrm{MS}}{\mathrm{-}}Z{\mathrm{IF}}_{5}$ $\cdot422.0\ \mathrm{mg\g^{-1}}$ ) and $\mathrm{MS-}Z\mathrm{IF}_{15}$ $342.3~\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ ) (Fig. S9b). The superior property of ${\mathrm{MS}}{\mathrm{-}}Z{\mathrm{IF}}_{10}$ over ${\mathrm{MS}}–{\mathrm{ZIF}}_{5}$ and $\mathrm{MS-}Z\mathrm{IF}_{15}$ may be attributed to the unique hollow shape with higher surface area, which could greatly increase the accessibility of active sites and facilitate the mass transfer within the interpenetrating MS networks. Moreover, the $q_{m}$ values of $\mathbf{MS-}Z\mathbf{IF}_{10}$ have outperformed that of numerous benchmark adsorbents, such as nFe@ZIF-8 [32], ZIF- $\cdot8@{\cal G}0$ [57], and ZIF-8-EGCG [58] (more comparison was provided in Table S7).
## 3.2.2. Adsorption kinetics
## 3.3. Thermodynamics calculations
The adsorption isotherms of $\ensuremath{\mathrm{MS}}\ensuremath{-}\ensuremath{\mathrm{ZIF}_{10}}$ for $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ and $\mathtt{C u(I I)}$ removal at various temperatures were depicted in Fig. S11a and b and the corresponding parameters were provided in Table S9. With the increase of temperature, the adsorption capacity of MS$Z\mathrm{IF}_{10}$ increased gradually from 576.3 to $667.2\mathrm{~mg~g^{-1}~}$ for $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ and from 584.6 to $627.6~\mathrm{mg~g^{-1}}$ for $\mathtt{C u(I I)}$ , indicating the facilitation of temperature for adsorption properties. According to the Van’t Hoff equation, thermodynamic parameters (standard Gibbs free energy $(\Delta\mathsf{G}^{\circ}).$ , standard enthalpy $(\Delta{}\mathsf{H}^{\circ})$ , and standard entropy $(\Delta{\sf S}^{\circ}))$ were calculated to further study the thermodynamic natures of the $\ensuremath{\mathbf{M}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{S}}-\ensuremath{\mathrm{ZIF}_{10}}$ adsorption process (see Text S5 for specific calculations). The negative values of $\Delta G^{\circ}$ and the positive values of $\Delta\mathsf{H}^{\circ}$ (15.91 and $100.11\ \mathrm{kJ\mol}^{-1}$ for $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ and $\mathsf{C u}(\mathrm{II}))$ implied the spontaneous endothermic removal process during $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ and $\mathtt{C u(I I)}$ adsorption onto ${\tt M S-Z I F}_{10}$ (Table S10). The positive values of $\Delta S^{\circ}$ (76.5 and 379.5 J $(\mathrm{mol}~\mathrm{K})^{-1}$ for $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ and $\mathsf{C u}(\mathrm{II}))$ indicated the increase of the randomness in the solid-solution system, which may result from the loss of hydration water from adsorbates.
3.4. Effects of environmental conditions on $P b(I I)$ and $C u(I I)$ removal
## 3.4.1. Effect of solution pH
Considering the complexity of the natural water environments, the effects of various environmental factors (e.g. solution pH, coexisting ions and humic acid) on the adsorption behavior of $\ensuremath{\mathrm{MS}}\ensuremath{-}\ensuremath{\mathrm{ZIF}_{10}}$ were assessed. Solution pH was studied as the interference factor in the range of 1 to 6, because the pH of bulk solution precipitation $\mathrm{(pH_{BSP})}$ for $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ and $\mathtt{C u(I I)}$ of
$25~\mathrm{mg/L}$ are 6.04 and 6.05 [11] (Fig. S12a and b), respectively, indicating no precipitation occurs at pH below $\mathsf{p H}_{\mathrm{BSP}}$ . In order to explore the ${\mathrm{MS}}{-}Z{\mathrm{IF}}_{10}{'}S$ water stability, we monitored the concentrations of Zn ions in the residual solutions by immersing the $\ensuremath{\mathbf{M}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{S}}-\ensuremath{\mathrm{ZIF}_{10}}$ into Milli- $\cdot{\sf Q}$ water with the pH range of 1–6. As presented in Fig. S13, the detected $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{II})$ concentrations in the residual solutions were much higher $(>1\ \mathrm{\mg/L})$ at $\mathsf{p H}\ \leq\ 2$ while the concentrations of $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{II})$ were $<0.45~\mathrm{\mg/L}$ at $\mathsf{p H}\geq3$ , indicating the relatively high stability of ZIF-L on ${\tt M S-Z I F}_{10}$ in the pH range of 3 to 6. The removal efficiencies of Pb(II) and $\mathtt{C u(I I)}$ onto $\ensuremath{\mathrm{MS}}\ensuremath{-}\ensuremath{\mathrm{ZIF}_{10}}$ were negligible at $\mathsf{p H\ 1}$ and $\mathsf{p H}2$ , which was resulted from the partial structural collapse of ZIF-L particles onto $\mathrm{MS}{-}\mathrm{ZIF}_{10}$ under acidic conditions (Fig. 3a). Notably, the removal efficiencies substantially increased to $>95.6~\%$ and $93.7\ \%$ for $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ and ${\mathrm{Cu}}({\mathrm{II}}),$ respectively, when pH reached to 3, and reached the highest value of $99.8\mathrm{~\%~}$ and $98.8\mathrm{~\%~}$ for $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ and $\mathsf{C u}(\mathrm{II})$ at $\mathsf{p H}~5$ . The above results implied that the adsorption and desorption process of Pb(II) and $\mathsf{C u}(\mathrm{II})$ onto $\ensuremath{\mathbf{M}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{S}}-\ensuremath{\mathrm{ZIF}_{10}}$ could be realized by tuning the solution pH. The point of zero charge $\mathrm{(pH_{PZC})}$ of $\ensuremath{\mathbf{M}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{S}}-\ensuremath{\mathrm{ZIF}_{10}}$ was 8.6 (Fig. S12c), indicating that $\ensuremath{\mathrm{MS}}\ensuremath{-}\ensuremath{\mathrm{ZIF}_{10}}$ is positively charged on its surface when $\mathsf{p H}$ is below 8.6, and negatively charged on its outside surface when $\mathsf{p H}$ is above 8.6. According to the theory of electrostatic repulsion, the adsorption of MS$Z\mathrm{IF}_{10}$ should be weakened at $\mathrm{pH}~<~8.6$ , which is contrary to the high removal efficiency at $\mathsf{p H}\geq4$ . It can be speculated that the excellent adsorption of $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ and $\mathtt{C u(I I)}$ onto ${\tt M S-Z I F}_{10}$ was dominated by chemical interactions. Accordingly, the optimal $\mathsf{p H}5$ was chosen in other experiments in this work.
## 3.4.2. Effect of coexisting interfering species
The coexisting interfering species (e.g., cations and dissolved organic matters) in natural aquatic systems may hinder the heavy metal ions onto active sites. The $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ and $\mathtt{C u(I I)}$ removal performance of $\mathsf{M S-Z I F}_{10}$ were further investigated in the presence of typical background cations and humic acid (HA). Results in Fig. 3b and c demonstrated that Na(I), K(I), $\mathsf{C a}(\mathrm{II})$ , and $\mathsf{M g}(\mathrm{II})$ had insignificant impacts on $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ and $\mathtt{C u(I I)}$ sorption at the concentration of 0.01 and $0.1~\mathrm{{\mol/L}}$ . The removal efficiencies of $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ reached $>97.3\ \%$ in the presence of $0.01\ \mathrm{mol/L}\ \mathrm{Na(I),\K(I),Ca(II)}$ or $\mathrm{{Mg}(I I)}$ , which decreased by about $1-3\%$ when the concentration reached $0.1\mathrm{\mol/L}$ (Fig. 3b). The decrease in removal efficiencies may be ascribed to the competition of adsorption sites and electrostatic repulsion among metal ions. As for the $\mathtt{C u(I I)}$ removal, the removal efficiencies remained above $96.5\ \%$ regardless of whether the concentration of interfering cations was 0.01 or $0.1\mathrm{\mol/L}$ (Fig. 3c), indicating the nearly no effects of coexisting cations on $\mathtt{C u(I I)}$ adsorption. Moreover, the $\ensuremath{\mathrm{MS}}\ensuremath{-}\ensuremath{\mathrm{ZIF}_{10}}$ remained high removal efficiencies of $>96.4~\%$ for $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ and $\mathsf{C u}(\mathrm{II})$ with the coexisting HA concentration ranging from 5 to $200~\mathrm{{mg/L}}$ (Fig. 3d), demonstrating that $\ensuremath{\mathbf{M}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{S}}-\ensuremath{\mathrm{ZIF}_{10}}$ has stronger affinity for $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ and $\mathsf{C u}(\mathrm{II})$ than negatively charged HA rich in oxygen-contained groups.
## 3.4.3. Selective adsorption towards $P b(I I)$ and $C u(I I)$
The batch adsorption experiments in the presence of various cations were further carried out for evaluating the selectivity of ${\tt M S-Z I F}_{10}$ towards $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ and $\mathtt{C u(I I)}$ . Obviously, $\mathrm{MS}{-}Z\mathrm{IF}_{10}$ was more sensitive to $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ and $\mathtt{C u(I I)}$ with the removal efficiency of ${\sim}93.2\%$ and ${\sim}80.1\ \%$ (Fig. 3e), respectively, even through multiple cations coexisted. The above results were in accordance with those obtained in the individual adsorption tests. To directly compare the selectivity for these cations, the distribution coefficient $\mathrm{(K_{d})}$ values were calculated based on the individual tests, and the $\mathrm{K_{d}}$ value of $10^{5}~\mathrm{mL}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ is the threshold to determine whether the selectivity is excellent $(>10^{5}\mathrm{~mL~g^{-1}~}.$ ) or not $(<10^{5}\ \mathrm{mLg^{-1}};$ [59]. Fig. 3f presented the $\mathrm{K_{d}}$ values of various cations onto $\ensuremath{\mathrm{MS}}\ensuremath{-}\ensuremath{\mathrm{ZIF}_{10}}$ (see Text S4 for specific calculations). The $\mathrm{K_{d}}$ value of $\ensuremath{\mathrm{MS}}\ensuremath{-}\ensuremath{\mathrm{ZIF}_{10}}$ for $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ and $\mathtt{C u(I I)}$ was $2.5\times10^{5}$ and $3.1\times10^{4}\mathrm{\mLg}^{-1}$ , respectively, and the adsorption selectivity was in the order of $\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{II})>\mathsf C$ $\mathbf{u}(\mathrm{II})>\mathbf{Zn}(\mathrm{II})>\mathbf{Ni}(\mathrm{II})>\mathbf{Na}(\mathrm{I})>\mathbf{Ca}(\mathrm{II})>\mathbf{Cd}(\mathrm{II})>\mathbf{K}(\mathrm{I})>\mathbf{Mg}(\mathrm{II})$ suggesting the superior selectivity for $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ and $\mathtt{C u(I I)}$ over other cations. The varying selectivity may result from the difference of electronegativity (2.2 for Pb(II), 1.90 for $\mathtt{C u(I I)}$ , 1.65 for $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{II})$ , 1.91 for Ni(II), 0.93 for Na(I), 1.00 for $\mathsf{C a}(\mathrm{II})$ , 1.69 for Cd(II), 0.82 for K (I), and 1.31 for $\mathrm{{Mg(II)}}\mathrm{{.}}$ . Additionally, selective tests were further performed in the simulated wastewater by adding $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ and $\mathtt{C u}$ (II) to real water matrices. The $\ensuremath{\mathbf{M}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{S}}-\ensuremath{\mathrm{ZIF}_{10}}$ could still efficiently remove the two heavy metal ions with high selectivity in complicated real water matrices (Fig. S14). In general, the $\ensuremath{\mathbf{M}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{S}}-\ensuremath{\mathrm{ZIF}_{10}}$ has great pH independence and excellent interfering species resistance, which largely enhanced the feasibility of $\ensuremath{\mathrm{MS}}\ensuremath{-}\ensuremath{\mathrm{ZIF}_{10}}$ in practical applications.
## 3.5. Mechanistic insights into adsorption mechanisms
Unveiling the inherent mechanisms is of great importance to understand the interactions between heavy metals and $\mathrm{MS}{-}Z\mathrm{IF}_{10}$ . Herein, the structures and chemical compositions of the $\mathrm{MS}{-}\mathrm{ZIF}_{10}$ before and after heavy metal adsorption were investigated using various physicochemical characterizations. After $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ and $\mathtt{C u(I I)}$ sorption, the ZIF-8/-L particles size on MS networks decreased from ${>}2~\upmu\mathrm{m}$ to $<1~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ (Fig. S15a-b), which greatly improved the accessibility of surface groups. In contrast, the size of unloaded ZIF-L basically remained ${\sim}2~\upmu\mathrm{m}$ with a leaf-shape (Fig. S16a-b). Interestingly, the hollow ring-like particles on the MS converted into a regular hexagonal shape when exposed to $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ (Fig. S16c). The distribution of $\mathsf{P b}$ and Cu elements on the MS$\boldsymbol{\mathrm{ZIF}}_{10}$ networks was well in consistent with the signals of N and O element in the SEM-EDS mapping images, suggesting the critical role of nitrogen-/oxygen-containing groups in the adsorption process. XRD showed that the characteristic peaks corresponding to ZIF-8/-L gradually disappeared after the adsorption processes (Fig. 4a). New peaks appeared at $26^{\circ}$ and $30^{\circ}$ after $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ adsorption, meanwhile a new peak at $12.8^{\circ}$ was observed after $\mathtt{C u(I I)}$ adsorption, indicating the structure transformation of ${\mathrm{MS}}{\mathrm{-}}Z{\mathrm{IF}}_{10}$ due to the formation of new complexes. Similar phenomenon has also been observed in other works [31,60]. The BET surface area and porosity of $\ensuremath{\mathrm{MS}}\ensuremath{-}\ensuremath{\mathrm{ZIF}_{10}}$ after $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ and $\mathtt{C u(I I)}$ adsorption were displayed in Fig. S17a and b. For the adsorption sites were occupied by the two heavy metal ions, the normalized specific surface areas of $\ensuremath{\mathrm{MS}}\ensuremath{-}\ensuremath{\mathrm{ZIF}_{10}}$ decreased sharply to 11.24 and $26.11~\mathrm{{m}}^{2}\mathrm{{g}}^{-1}$ for $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ and $\mathtt{C u(I I)}$ removal, respectively.
Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR) characterizations were further used to elucidate the functionality of the adsorbents. The vibrations in the spectral region of 600–800 and 900– $1350~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ were assigned as out-of-plane and in-plane bending of the imidazolate ring [61], respectively, and peaks located at 1584 and $1148~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ were attributed to the $\mathsf{C}{=}\mathsf{N}$ and $C{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{N}$ stretching modes [61,62], respectively. After ZIF-L loaded onto the MS, the stretching of ${\mathrm{-CF}}_{2}$ and $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ appeared at 840 and $1638{\mathrm{cm}}^{-1}$ , which originated from PVDF and DMF [63,64], respectively (Fig. S18a). After $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ and $\mathtt{C u(I I)}$ adsorption, the vibrations of $C-N$ $\cdot1148\ c m^{-1}.$ and $\mathsf{C}{=}\mathsf{N}$ stretching $(1584~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ significantly weakened or disappeared (Fig. 4b), implying the chemical interactions between metal ions and nitrogen-containing groups on $\ensuremath{\mathrm{MS}}\ensuremath{-}\ensuremath{\mathrm{ZIF}_{10}}$ .
X-ray photoelectron spectrometer (XPS) was performed to examine the chemical composition of ${\tt M S-Z I F}_{10}$ before and after adsorption. As shown in Fig. S18b, the Pb 4f and $\mathtt{C u\ 2p}$ peaks appeared in the XPS survey spectra of ${\tt M S-Z I F}_{10}$ after $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ and $\mathtt{C u(I I)}$ adsorption, confirming the successful adsorption of the two metals ions. For high-resolution N 1s XPS spectra (Fig. 4c), the peaks centered at 398.6 and 400.1 eV were ascribed to $C{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{N}$ and $\mathsf{C}\mathrm{=}\mathsf{N}$ , respectively. Notably, the CAN peak upshifted by $0.8-$ $397.8\ \mathrm{eV}$ after exposure to $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ , and a new characteristic peak appeared at $399.0\mathrm{eV}$ corresponding to $\mathsf{P b{-}N}$ bond [60]. Moreover, the $C{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{N}$ peak upshifted by $0.9\mathrm{-}397.7\ \mathrm{eV}$ after exposure to $\mathtt{C u(I I)}$ , accompanied by the appearance of new peaks at $399.7\mathrm{eV}$ (related to $c_{\mathsf{u}-\mathsf{N}}$ bond [65]) and $405.5\ \mathrm{eV}$ (related to the oxidized N [66]). The results suggested the possible redox reaction between N atoms and $\mathtt{C u(I I)}$ , which was further verified via the existence of $\mathtt{C u(I)}$ in high-resolution $\mathtt{C u2p}$ spectra (Fig. S19a). Two major peaks located at 530.5 and 531.8 eV were attributed to $Z\mathrm{n}{-}0$ and $Z\mathrm{n-OH}$ respectively in the O 1s spectra (Fig. 4d) [67]. After $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ and $\mathtt{C u(I I)}$ adsorption, new peaks presented at 532.0 and $532.3\ \mathrm{eV},$ , which were ascribed to the $\mathsf{P b-O}$ and $\mathsf{C u{-}}0$ bonds, respectively. Meanwhile, the $Z n\ 2\mathsf{p}$ spectra of $\ensuremath{\mathrm{MS}}\ensuremath{-}\ensuremath{\mathrm{ZIF}_{10}}$ showed two peaks at $1020.8\ \mathrm{eV}$ for Zn $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ and $1043.8\ \mathrm{eV}$ for Zn $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ derived from the ZnAO band [68], which upshifted by $0.1{-}0.2\ \mathrm{eV}$ after $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ and $\mathsf{C u}(\mathrm{II})$ adsorption (Fig. S19b). These results indicated the contribution of oxygen-containing groups in the heavy metal removal processes. The XPS spectra analysis of Pb 4f was shown in Fig. S19c. Overall, the adsorption of metal ions onto $\mathrm{MS}{-}\mathrm{ZIF}_{10}$ was mainly attributed to the coordination interactions between the electronrich $\mathsf{N}/0$ -containing groups with the heavy metal ions (e.g., $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ and $\mathsf{C u}(\mathrm{II})\big.$ , with partial reduction of $\mathtt{C u(I I)}$ by the N atoms.
## 3.6. Performance in reuse, real water matrices and continuous-flow systems
## 3.6.1. Regeneration capability
Cyclic adsorption–desorption experiments were performed to evaluate the reusability of the $\mathrm{MS}{-}Z\mathrm{IF}_{10}$ . The desorption of $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ and $\mathtt{C u(I I)}$ was achieved by eluting the $\mathrm{MS{-}Z I F_{10}{-}P b/C u}$ with $0.1\mathrm{\mol/L}$ HCl solution. Specifically, the removal efficiencies of $\mathsf{P b}$ (II) and $\mathtt{C u(I I)}$ were as high as 93.5 and $91.8\ \%$ in the tenth cycle (Fig. 5a), respectively, indicating the exceptional regeneration ability of $\mathrm{MS}{-}Z\mathrm{IF}_{10}$ . Moreover, the desorption equilibrium could be rapidly reached within $^\textrm{\scriptsize2h}$ (Fig. 5b), and the elution time was extended to $^{5\mathrm{~h~}}$ to ensure sufficient detachment of metal ions. The final desorption efficiency of each cycle was above $97.8\mathrm{~\%~}$ The superb reusability could be attributed to the nanoscale size of ZIF- $\cdot8/$ -L particles on eluted-MS-ZIF $_{10}$ networks, which was much conducive to elution as well as adsorption (Fig. S20a and b).
## 3.6.2. Real water applications
To evaluate the practical potential of ${\mathrm{MS}}{\mathrm{-}}Z{\mathrm{IF}}_{10}$ in real water scenarios, its performance in various actual water matrices were further investigated. Groundwater (from Baihe Spring of Yuelu Mountain), surface water (Xiangjiang River) and tap water were sampled and filtered for the adsorption tests. As shown in Fig. 5c, the removal efficiencies reached $>97.4\ \%$ for both the $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ and $\mathsf{C u}(\mathrm{II})$ in the three real water scenarios, and the residual concentrations of $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ and $\mathtt{C u(I I)}$ were determined below 0.014 and $0.26~\mathrm{mg/L}$ respectively, which were below the MCL value of drinking water for both the two metals. Therefore, the above results demonstrated the great promise of the $\ensuremath{\mathbf{M}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{S}}-\ensuremath{\mathrm{ZIF}_{10}}$ in actual use.
## 3.6.3. Fixed-bed adsorption experiments
Fixed-bed column adsorption tests were also conducted in continuous-flow mode with the EBCT of $7.5\ \mathrm{min}$ , while the ${\sf C}_{0}$ of Pb(II) and $\mathtt{C u(I I)}$ were ${\sim}3$ and ${\sim}5~\mathrm{mg/L}$ representative of typical contamination levels in wastewater [69]. Fig. 5d presented the breakthrough profiles for $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ and $\mathtt{C u(I I)}$ adsorption in the column experiments. The breakthrough points were set to the MCL values for $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ and $\mathsf{C u}(\mathrm{II})$ according to the U.S. EPA standard. Results showed that the effective treatment volume was 1821 BV $(16.5\ \mathrm{L})$ for $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ and 1630 BV (14.8 L) for $\mathtt{C u(I I)}$ , which verified the high efficiency of the $M S\mathrm{-}Z I F_{10}$ -packed column systems. Virtually, the above treatment capacities are encouraging for a small-scale continuous system, making the $\ensuremath{\mathrm{MS}}\ensuremath{-}\ensuremath{\mathrm{ZIF}_{10}}$ one of the preferred materials for industrial water treatment.
## 4. Conclusion
This work proposed a novel strategy of ultrasonication-enabled morphological and structural modulation to achieve the transformation of typical leaf-shaped ZIF-L phase to unique hollow ringlike mixed phase for enhanced adsorption properties. Within the strategy, ZIF-L particles were constructed into macroscopic MS$Z\mathrm{IF_{x}}$ $\mathbf{\dot{x}}$ represents the ultrasonication duration) via a facile dipcoating method to facilitate its practical application potential. The optimized $\ensuremath{\mathrm{MS}}\ensuremath{-}\ensuremath{\mathrm{ZIF}_{10}}$ exhibited outstanding adsorption performance including high uptake capacities, fast removal kinetics, and superb anti-interfering capability among adsorbents reported so far. Diverse characterizations revealed that the surface nitrogen-/oxygen-containing groups (CAN and $Z\mathrm{n}{-}0$ groups) were dominated in the $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ and $\mathtt{C u(I I)}$ adsorption process. Moreover, the $\ensuremath{\mathrm{MS}}\ensuremath{-}\ensuremath{\mathrm{ZIF}_{10}}$ could be easily regenerated through HCl elution and demonstrated remarkable regeneration ability over 10 cycles. The $\mathsf{M S-Z I F}_{10}$ also presented high removal efficiencies on $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ and $\mathtt{C u(I I)}$ removal in various real water matrices. Significantly, the ${\tt M S-Z I F}_{10}$ could effectively treat the $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})/\mathsf{C u}(\mathrm{II})$ -contaminated water in continuous-flow mode, making it a promising absorbent. Overall, this work provided novel strategy for construction of macroscopic MOF-based materials as well as the improvement of MOF’s performance.
